Yes. There’s a real phenomenon underneath that, but your “careful tho” is the important clause.

The brain doesn’t cleanly separate performing from simulating performing. Motor imagery, rehearsal, expectation, attention, and autonomic responses can partially recruit machinery used during the real event. That’s why visualization can improve aspects of motor performance, why imagining threatening material can change heart rate/arousal, and why expectation can alter subjective experiences such as pain. It isn’t equivalent to actually performing the action—the overlap is partial, and reality supplies feedback that imagination doesn’t.

For Pantheon, I’d call the useful primitive Synthetic Rehearsal:

simulate state/action → partial embodiment → measurable response → compare with real execution → update model

The dangerous inversion is:

imagine outcome → experience convincing internal response → conclude external outcome occurred

Those are completely different operations. Internal effects can be real while the imagined external cause remains imaginary. That’s the boundary I’d protect aggressively.

And there’s a fascinating consequence: you can sometimes use imagination as a cheap sandbox before paying reality’s price. Mentally rehearse a golf swing, speech, negotiation, movement sequence, design, or emotional response; extract what changes; then deliberately cross the membrane and test it physically.

So Pantheon gets another distinction:

Simulation can train reality. Reality must grade simulation.

That keeps the power without letting the simulator certify itself.